// probes/rounding/tolerance-refuses.kdl -- how the 0.001pt tolerance is added.
//
// The frame is 11.001pt tall and the title takes 10, so 1.001pt remains and
// the detail band asks for 1.002 -- one thousandth over.  Whether that fits
// is the whole question, and the two readings of "a tolerance of 0.001pt"
// answer it differently: `1.002 <= 1.001 + 0.001` is false in binary64,
// because the addition gives 1.0019999999999998, while comparing thousandths
// as integers makes it 1002 against 1001 + 1 and admits it.
//
// The reference ejects: two pages, the detail band alone on the second.
// So the tolerance is an addition in binary64 like the coordinates around it.
//
// `tolerance-admits` is the same construction one thousandth over a remaining
// 20.003pt, where the addition does land exactly and the band fits.  The pair
// is what shows the answer depends on the magnitude, which is what rules the
// integer reading out rather than merely leaving it untested.  The dependence
// is on the binade rather than on the size: the addition falls short for half
// the three-decimal values in [1, 2), for none at all in [4, 8), [16, 32) or
// [64, 256), and for 22.5% of them across the 842pt of an A4 page.
//
// doc/layout.md#coordinates-and-rounding is what this became.

report name="rounding/tolerance-refuses" {
  font "body" file="../../../../example/fonts/Go-Regular.ttf" size=10
  layout width=200 height=11.001 leftmargin=0 rightmargin=0 topmargin=0 bottommargin=0 {
    style font="body" color="black"
    title height=10 {
      rectangle left=0 top=0 width=1 height=5
    }
    detail height=1.002 {
      rectangle left=0 top=0 width=1 height=1
    }
  }
}
